#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162850} :activity/id #uuid "fbe58342-4666-5afb-a7ae-33c1d4a9914d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050972 :topic/id #uuid "7685a107-6df6-5dd2-9d7d-cacd4a8d7f80" :topic/math-academy-id 3121} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162850 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163225 :tutorial/content "Let $n$ be a positive integer. A  function \n\n$$\nf: \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nis a **linear form**  (or  *linear functional*) if there is a row-vector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that for any $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={a}_{1}{x}_{1}+{a}_{2}{x}_{2}+…+{a}_{n}{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, let $f(\\mathbf{x})$ be the linear form   defined on $\\Bbb R^2$ by $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{x},$ where $[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \n\nSuppose we want to evaluate $f(\\mathbf{x})$ at $[\\begin{aligned}\\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we simply substitute $[\\begin{aligned}\\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]$ and obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\,1 \\\\ \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a0cf8344-4180-5121-8429-bf2232ea1c94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4883 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10801c67-6977-5b0e-8ebd-4c3a8edd48f7" :step/math-academy-id 30534 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161980 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161772 :question/id #uuid "59ea7ebb-5b69-5551-a5b9-c286e2730881" :question/math-academy-id "e-8160"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b58f4ff-b6ed-5df6-b04f-137e285d5460" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357604}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Linear Form"} :step/id #uuid "a5a71a2d-8697-5b95-99ea-19befec73017" :step/math-academy-id 30561 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163234 :tutorial/content "Let $n$ be a positive integer. A  function $B: \\Bbb R^n\\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R$ is said to be a **bilinear form** if there is an $n\\times n$   matrix $A$ such that \n\n$$\nB(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) =  \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{y}, \\qquad  \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\Bbb R^n.\n$$\n\nSuppose, for example, that $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ To  evaluate the bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{y}$ when $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ we substitute  these vectors in the above expression as follows \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo write down our bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ above in terms of vector components \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe proceed as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(3{y}_{1}+4{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c47b7403-36bf-5bf1-b9ab-46fa073f3659" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4896 :tutorial/title "Bilinear Forms"} :step/id #uuid "4a0b1af7-0002-5905-8ed2-197ac9436ab0" :step/math-academy-id 30562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161981 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161773 :question/id #uuid "30204c69-3362-5cc1-a04a-3bb83559b300" :question/math-academy-id "e-8161"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7083d465-9871-59ca-97fa-583e740e961f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357607}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Bilinear Form"} :step/id #uuid "b118bcc0-126e-5a1f-a269-8e6c781977ad" :step/math-academy-id 30563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162863}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161982 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161774 :question/id #uuid "34398117-07f4-51d9-a879-30a0f6891e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-8162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55277d69-eedc-5c5c-883a-083746e00947" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357615}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Bilinear Form Given Its Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d8e4413d-393f-5d07-acd6-7fa0e9f24c33" :step/math-academy-id 30564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162864}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161983 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161775 :question/id #uuid "84e1b597-45d7-51f4-aaa9-8a52c753a510" :question/math-academy-id "e-8163"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f60cd05d-e4af-5bf1-8827-cab48da934f1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357638}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Bilinear Form"} :step/id #uuid "171e7c1e-5d10-5f94-a034-21a27c1aa8d8" :step/math-academy-id 30565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162865}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163235 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. Then, it readily follows that the function $B: \\Bbb R^n\\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R$ defined as \n\n$$\nB(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) =  \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\, \\mathbf{y}, \\qquad \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\Bbb R^n,\n$$\n\nis **linear in each argument** separately. That's the reason why we call them *bilinear*.\n\n- For $\\mathbf{y}$ fixed, all $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\Bbb R^n,$  and any real constant $c$, linearity in the first argument means the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(u+v,y) & =B(u,y)+B(v,y) \\\\ B(cu,y) & =c\\,B(u,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $\\mathbf{x}$ fixed, all $\\mathbf{w}, \\mathbf{z} \\in \\Bbb R^n,$ and any real constant $c$, linearity in the second argument means the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,w+z) & =B(x,w)+B(x,z) \\\\ B(x,cw) & =c\\,B(x,w)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "587cb24c-48a7-5ba8-9424-4dae3dd192d9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4898 :tutorial/title "Properties of Bilinear Forms"} :step/id #uuid "d20da724-d87e-59ed-8fed-cc45889fcaea" :step/math-academy-id 30572}] :activity/title "Bilinear Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162217}]}
